<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01245#assertion_about_01245> <http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#hasToxicityExplanation> "The meme uses the juxtaposition of a child in military attire with the context of a nativity play to create a humorous or ironic commentary, implying that the child's participation in a religious event was met with disapproval or exclusion. While not explicitly abusive or hateful, the visual and textual contrast may be perceived as harmful by some, as it potentially reinforces negative stereotypes about military imagery in religious contexts or suggests that children dressed in such attire are unwelcome in sacred spaces. The meme relies on visual and textual juxtaposition for comedic effect, but the underlying message could be interpreted as subtly devaluing the child's participation or implying that his attire is inappropriate or offensive in a religious setting, which may cause discomfort or harm to individuals who value religious inclusivity or see the child's attire as a symbol of non-conformity."@en .
